Extend the lookbehind to exclude a preceding quote, backslash and apostrophe, e.g. `(?<![A-Za-z0-9_"\'])`, which removes all 86 spurious matches on the current tree. That is a mitigation, not a proof — a regex cannot correctly tokenise Rust string literals, and the two literal forms are mutually recursive (an ordinary literal can contain `r"`, a raw literal can contain `"`). The robust fix is a single left-to-right character scanner that walks the file once, tracking whether it is in code / line comment / block comment / ordinary literal / raw literal with N hashes, and blanking non-code spans — that also picks up `/* ... */` block comments, which neither the old nor the new code handles.

Add a test that pins the escaped-CR case specifically (assert `_extract_cfg_expressions('let s = "\r";
#[cfg(not(feature = "f"))]
fn g() {}
') == ['not(feature = "f")']`), and consider an invariant test over the real crates/ tree asserting that sanitisation never reduces the count of extracted cfg expressions in a file containing no cfg-like text inside any literal.
